<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Finnish War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Finnish War was fought from 1808 to 1809 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, which invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> after Sweden refused to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a>. Russian forces took the fortress of Sveaborg and overran Finland. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fredrikshamn">Treaty of Fredrikshamn</a> of 1809 Sweden ceded Finland, which became an autonomous grand duchy under the Russian tsar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finnish-war.jpg" alt="Finnish War" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imonoz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finnish_War_Collage_5.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After concluding the Treaty of Tilsit with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1807, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a> wrote on 24 September 1807 to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iv-adolf">Gustav IV Adolf</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> that peace between them depended on Sweden accepting the treaty's limits, which in practice meant cutting off trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The king, who viewed Napoleon as the Antichrist, secured an alliance with Britain on 8 February 1808. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> his stubbornness was seen as a convenient pretext for occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. Swedish plans assumed that war in winter was impossible, while Russian planners chose a winter offensive, when frozen seas would isolate Finland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 21 February 1808, 24,000 Russian troops under Friedrich Wilhelm von Buxhoeveden crossed the border and took Loviisa; war was not declared until April. Helsinki fell on 2 March and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turku">Turku</a> on 21 March, as the Swedish army under General Wilhelm Mauritz Klingspor withdrew northwards. Sveaborg, with a garrison of 6,000 men and over 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, surrendered on 6 May 1808 after prolonged negotiations, its commander Admiral Carl Olof Cronstedt believing resistance futile, and the Russians gained most of the Swedish archipelago fleet intact. Swedish counter-attacks at Siikajoki and Revolax halted the offensive for a time, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> fighting.</p><p>Reinforced to 55,000 men against an estimated 36,000, the Russians attacked again in August, and Count Nikolay Kamensky won the most important victory at Oravais on 14 September. By November 1808 Russian forces had overrun all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, and the Convention of Olkijoki of 19 November obliged the Swedish army to leave. In March 1809 Russian columns crossed the frozen Gulf of Bothnia, occupying the Åland Islands and entering Umeå on 24 March. King Gustav IV, blamed for the loss of Finland, was dethroned by a coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> on 13 March, and his uncle became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xiii-of-sweden">Charles XIII of Sweden</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fredrikshamn">Treaty of Fredrikshamn</a> of 17 September 1809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> also gave up its domains east of the Torne river. The Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> kept the Gustavian constitution of 1772 with only slight modifications until 1919, and in 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> attached to it the areas it had gained from Sweden in the 18th century, known as Old Finland. In Sweden the war was followed by a new constitution and, in 1818, by the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bernadotte">House of Bernadotte</a>. Two studies of 2015 by the political scientists Jan Teorell and Bo Rothstein argue that the defeat moved Swedish elites to reform the bureaucracy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Finnish War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wars against Russia in 1741–1743 and 1788–1790 failed to reverse the verdict, and in the <b>Finnish War</b> of 1808–1809 Russia conquered Finland, ceded by the Treaty of Fredrikshamn in 1809; the defeat led to the deposition of Gustav IV Adolf.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Russia conquered Finland from Sweden in the <b>Finnish War</b> of 1808-1809.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-finland">Grand Duchy of Finland</a></li></ul></section>
